DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppbbmmtteexxttppss takes a single line of text from the command line and renders
       it into a PBM image.  The image is of a single line  of  text;  newline
       characters in the input have no effect.

       See  ppbbmmtteexxtt for a more sophisticated generator of text, but using less
       common font formats.  ppbbmmtteexxtt can generate multiple lines of text.

       The --ppllaaiinn common option ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩  has no effect  be‐
       fore Netpbm 10.42 (March 2008).  The output is always raw PBM.


   MMaarrggiinnss
       By  default,  the image is cropped at the top and the right.  It is not
       cropped at the left or bottom so that the text begins at the same posi‐
       tion  relative  to the origin.  The size of the default left and bottom
       margins is explained below.

       You can set whatever margin you want with options --lleeffttmmaarrggiinn,  --rriigghhtt‐‐
       mmaarrggiinn,  --ttooppmmaarrggiinn  and  --bboottttoommmmaarrggiinn.  The specified amount of white
       space gets added to the far edge of type, e.g. if you specify 10 points
       for --ttooppmmaarrggiinn, you will get 10 points of white space above the highest
       character on the line.  Specify 0 to crop a side.

       --aasscceenntt adds white space to the top to reach a specified distance above
       the  text  baseline,  and --ddeesscceenntt adds white space to to the bottom to
       reach a specified distance below the text baseline.

       --aasscceenntt and --ddeesscceenntt are more useful than --ttooppmmaarrggiinn  and  --bboottttoommaarrggiinn
       when  you  render  two  pieces of text (in separate invocations of ppbbmm‐‐
       tteexxttppss) that you will concatenate horizontally.  With --aasscceenntt and  --ddee‐‐
       sscceenntt, the two images will be the same height with the text baseline in
       the same place.  With --ttooppmmaarrggiinn and --bboottttoommmmaarrggiinn, that may not be the
       case.

       Example:

            $ pbmtextps -font=Times-Roman -descent=20 \
                 'The soup is called' > a1.pbm
            $ pbmtextps -font=Itallic -descent=20 'Goulash.' > a2.pbm
            $ pnmcat -lr -jb a1.pbm a2.pbm > out.pbm


       If  you have --aasscceenntt, there is probably no point in specifying --ttooppmmaarr‐‐
       ggiinn too, but if you do, the effect is cumulative.  The same is true  of
       --ddeesscceenntt and --bboottttoommmmaarrggiinn.

       --ppaadd  pads the image on the top and bottom to the where the highest and
       lowest characters in the font would reach, even if you don't have those
       characters  in your text.  This is useful if you will generate multiple
       images of text (with multiple invocations of ppbbmmtteexxttppss) and concatenate
       them  vertically to create a multiline text image.  --ppaadd makes sure the
       lines in this image are equally spaced.

       Example:

           $ pbmtextps 'cat'   | pamfile
           $ pbmtextps 'Catty' | pamfile


       The commands above, with no --ppaadd, show that the 'Catty' image is higher
       because capital C reaches high and 'y' reaches low.

           $ pbmtextps -pad 'cat'   | pamfile
           $ pbmtextps -pad 'Catty' | pamfile


       The  commands  above,  with  --ppaadd,  show  that both images are the same
       height.

       If you specify --ppaadd with --aasscceenntt or --ddeesscceenntt, the larger value  is  ef‐
       fective.

       --ccrroopp makes the program crop all sides to the far edge of the type.  It
       is the same as -leftmargin=0  -rightmargin=0  -topmargin=0  -bottommar‐
       gin=0.

       You cannot specify any other margin-affecting options with --ccrroopp.

       The  default  top margin, when you specify neither --aasscceenntt, --ttooppmmaarrggiinn,
       nor --ppaadd, is as if you specified ttooppmmaarrggiinn==00.

       The default bottom margin, when you specify neither --ddeesscceenntt,  --bboottttoomm‐‐
       mmaarrggiinn, nor --ppaadd, is as if you specified --ddeesscceenntt==_1_._5_*_f_o_n_t_s_i_z_e.

       The  default left margin, when you do not specify --lleeffttmmaarrggiinn, is as if
       you specified --lleeffttmmaarrggiinn==_0_._5_*_f_o_n_t_s_i_z_e.

       The default right margin, when you do not specify --rriigghhttmmaarrggiinn,  is  as
       if you specified --rriigghhttmmaarrggiinn==00.



OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In  addition  to  the options common to all programs based on libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), ppbbmmtteexxttppss recognizes  the
       following command line options:



       --ffoonntt==_f_o_n_t_n_a_m_e

              This  specifies  the  font  to use.  _f_o_n_t_n_a_m_e is the name of any
              valid postscript font which is installed on the system.

              The default is TTiimmeessRRoommaann.

              WWaarrnniinngg:: if _f_o_n_t_n_a_m_e does not name a valid font, ppbbmmtteexxttppss  just
              uses the default font.  It does not tell you it is doing this.


       --ffoonnttssiizzee==_f_l_o_a_t
              This is the size of the font in points.  See the --rreessoolluuttiioonn op‐
              tion for information on how to interpret this size.

              The default is 24 points.

              Before Netpbm 10.75 (June 2016), this has to be a whole number.


       --rreessoolluuttiioonn==_n
              This is the resolution in dots per inch of distance measurements
              pertaining  to  generation  of the image.  PBM images don't have
              any inherent resolution, so a distance such as "1 inch"  doesn't
              mean  anything  unless  you  separately  specify what resolution
              you're talking about.  That's what this option does.

              In particular, the meaning of the font  size  is  determined  by
              this  resolution.  If the font size is 24 points and the resolu‐
              tion is 150 dpi, then the font size is 50 pixels.

              The default is 150 dpi.


       --lleeffttmmaarrggiinn==_n

       --rriigghhttmmaarrggiinn==_n

       --ttooppmmaarrggiinn==_n

       --bboottttoommmmaarrggiinn==_n
              These options control the margins added to the  image,  measured
              from  the far edge of the type.  See Margins ⟨#margins⟩  for de‐
              tails.

              All sizes are in points, as a floating point number.

              These options were new in Netpbm 10.75 (June 2016).


       --aasscceenntt==_n

       --ddeesscceenntt==_n
              These options control the margins added to the  image,  measured
              from the text baseline.  See Margins ⟨#margins⟩  for details.

              Sizes are in points, as a floating point number.

              These options were new in Netpbm 10.75 (June 2016).


       --ppaadd   This pads the image on the top and bottom to the where the high‐
              est and lowest characters in the font would reach, even  if  you
              don't   have   those  characters  in  your  text.   See  Margins
              ⟨#margins⟩  for details.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.75 (June 2016).


       --ccrroopp  This makes the program crop all sides to the  far  edge  of  the
              type.   It  is the same as -leftmargin=0 -rightmargin=0 -topmar‐
              gin=0 -bottommargin=0.  See Margins ⟨#margins⟩  for details.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.75 (June 2016).


       --ssttrrookkee==_n
              This is the width of line, in points, to use  for  stroke  font.
              There  is  no  default  stroke  width because the characters are
              solid by default.


       --vveerrbboossee
              This option makes ppbbmmtteexxttppss display extra information  on  Stan‐
              dard Error about its processing.


       --dduummpp--ppss
              This  option  makes ppbbmmtteexxttppss write to Standard Output the Post‐
              script program it would use to create the image, rather than the
              image  itself.  You can use this as input to a Postscript inter‐
              preter (such as Ghostscript or a a printer) or to understand the
              program better.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.75 (June 2016).




UUSSAAGGEE
       You can generate antialiased text by using a larger resolution than the
       default and scaling the image down using ppaammssccaallee.

       See the manual for the similar ppbbmmtteexxtt for more advice on usage.


